// review-core — the shared multi-lens review sub-pipeline
// (review-pipeline-spec RV2).
//
// Five adversarial reviewers run in parallel, each from a different lens,
// then `synth` merges their findings into one review plus a PASS/FAIL
// verdict. Reused by the other pipelines via subgraph inlining,
// seeding `diff_cmd` through var.diff_cmd.
//
// The one input is `diff_cmd`: the command that shows the change under
// review. Each lens runs it to see the diff; different callers pass
// different diffs. Standalone:
//
//   attractor run review-core -var diff_cmd="jj diff"
//
// Findings ride context, not files: each lens sets output_key=review.<lens>
// so its full response is captured into its outcome's context_updates, which
// `parallel` bundles into parallel.results. `synth` interpolates
// $context.parallel.results, so nothing is written into the repo cwd and the
// diff under review stays clean.
digraph review_core {
    // D1: transient backend failures (429/5xx, stalls, network) retry
    // with backoff instead of killing the run.
    default_max_retries = 2
    goal = "Multi-lens review of the change shown by $context.diff_cmd"
    vars = "diff_cmd"
    max_node_visits = 2

    start [shape=Mdiamond]

    // Fan out to every lens concurrently. The branches converge on `synth`
    // (their only common downstream), so the parallel handler routes there
    // directly — no fan_in, which would only pick a single winner.
    fan_out [type="parallel"]

    // Models come entirely from the stylesheet (no inline pins). The `.review`
    // class covers all five lenses + synth; `correctness` also carries a
    // `correctness` class so the sheet can put it on a different model family
    // (e.g. a codex/GPT provider) for adversarial diversity — see
    // pipelines/models.css.
    correctness    [type="codergen", fidelity="truncate", class="review, lens, correctness", output_key="review.correctness", timeout="30m", prompt="Run `$context.diff_cmd` to see the change under review. Assume this code is broken and find the defects — edge cases, error paths, partial failures, races. Judge the CHANGE, not the codebase around it: before reporting anything, ask whether the same problem exists identically on the base branch today. If it does, this change did not introduce it, so say in one line that you checked and move on. For each real defect give the concrete inputs or sequence that trigger it and file:line evidence. A defect you can demonstrate beats three you can imagine."]
    design         [type="codergen", fidelity="truncate", class="review, lens", output_key="review.design", timeout="30m", prompt="Run `$context.diff_cmd` to see the change under review. Review from a clean-code perspective; focus on the coupling and cohesion of the modules. Call out every design smell."]
    prod_safety    [type="codergen", fidelity="truncate", class="review, lens", output_key="review.prod_safety", timeout="30m", prompt="Run `$context.diff_cmd` to see the change under review. Why should we not merge this to production? Judge the operational DELTA rather than absolute risk: for each risk, first ask whether it exists identically on the base branch today, and if it does, say so in one line and move on — this change is not the place to fix pre-existing gaps. What is left is what this change alters: failure modes under real data volumes, what happens on the first deploy and on rollback, whether an operator learns when it breaks, and behaviour during shutdown or restart. Give the concrete consequence and file:line evidence for each, and say whether it is worse than, the same as, or better than the base branch."]
    simplification [type="codergen", fidelity="truncate", class="review, lens", output_key="review.simplification", timeout="30m", prompt="Run `$context.diff_cmd` to see the change under review. How can we simplify this and make it less complected? List every concrete simplification."]
    tests          [type="codergen", fidelity="truncate", class="review, lens", output_key="review.tests", timeout="30m", prompt="Run `$context.diff_cmd` to see the change under review. Judge whether the tests would catch this change going wrong. Work from the failure back to the test: for each way this change could break, name the test that fails, or say there is none. Then invert — construct a way the code could be broken while every test here still passes; each construction you can build is a real gap, and if you cannot build one, say so. Flag tests coupled to how the code is written rather than what it does, since they break on the next refactor. Two hard rules. First, coverage of behaviour that predates this change is not this change's obligation. Second, if this change MODIFIES OR DELETES A PRE-EXISTING TEST, that is a finding and you must report it: pre-existing tests encode the current contract, and editing one to make a suite pass hides a regression instead of fixing it. Ask in every case why a passing suite needed an existing test changed, and say plainly if the honest answer is that the change broke it."]

    // Convergence node: merges the five findings and self-reports the
    // verdict via the status-file contract (spec §4.5). Its outcome IS
    // review-core's terminal outcome, so a parent can branch on it.
    synth [type="codergen", class="review, synth", require_status="true", timeout="15m", prompt="Five reviewers each reviewed the same change through a different lens. Their findings are in $context.parallel.results (JSON — each branch's context_updates holds one review.<lens> entry). Before including a finding, verify it against the actual code: the reviewers worked from a diff, you have the repository. Discard whatever is speculative or does not hold up. Before sorting, look through your context for the APPROVED PLAN for this change — earlier stages of this run may include one, and a human approved it at a gate. If you find one, it is authoritative about INTENT. Reviewers see only the diff, so they cannot tell a deliberate decision from an accident, and they will sometimes object to something that was chosen on purpose. Your job is to tell those apart: reviewers audit EXECUTION, the plan settles INTENT. A finding that objects to a decision the plan states is a NOTE, not a blocker — record it in the summary, name the decision it contradicts, and do not block on it. A finding that the code fails to do what the plan decided, or is wrong on its own terms, still blocks. If there is no plan in your context, judge the change on its own and use the rules below unchanged.

Then sort what survives. A finding BLOCKS only if it is a defect in the code this change touched, evidenced at file:line, that would produce wrong behaviour or a failure in production. A blocker is something the change got WRONG, never something the change DECIDED. Everything else is a note in the summary, including: a risk that exists identically on the base branch, a request for capability or coverage beyond this change's scope, a preference about how working code is written, and anything about which commit a change landed in. If you are unsure whether something blocks, it does not block — a note that turns out to matter costs a review comment, while a false block costs a rework round that degrades the change. Merge what survives into a single review, deduping overlapping points. Then self-report by writing `{stage_dir}/status.json` (that exact path): outcome `success` (lowercase) with context_updates {review.summary, review.verdict=pass} when nothing blocks, putting the non-blocking notes in review.summary; otherwise outcome `fail` (lowercase) with the blocking issues in failure_reason and context_updates {review.verdict=fail, review.summary}."]

    done [shape=Msquare]

    start -> fan_out

    fan_out -> correctness
    fan_out -> design
    fan_out -> prod_safety
    fan_out -> simplification
    fan_out -> tests

    correctness    -> synth
    design         -> synth
    prod_safety    -> synth
    simplification -> synth
    tests          -> synth

    synth -> done
}
